{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KaCC191gqmPxhz9Je5X1fk","type":"tool_result","content":"273\t## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n274\t\n275\t**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n276\t\n277\t### Requirement\n278\t\n279\tStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n280\t\n281\t### Verification recipe\n282\t\n283\tGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n284\t\n285\t**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n286\t\n287\t### Checks for likely_pass\n288\t\n289\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n290\t\n291\t- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n292\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n293\t- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n294\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n295\t- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n296\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n297\t\n298\t### Not covered here\n299\t\n300\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n301\t\n302\t- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n303\t- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n304\t- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n305\t\n306\t### Heuristics\n307\t\n308\t**Provable from code:** partly\n309\t\n310\t**Look for:**\n311\t- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n312\t- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n313\t- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n314\t- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n315\t\n316\t**Red flags:**\n317\t- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n318\t- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n319\t- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n320\t- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n321\t\n322\t**Where to look:**\n323\t- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n324\t- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n325\t- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n326\t- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n327\t\n328\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n329\t\n330\t**N/A when:** never\n331\t\n332\t## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n333\t\n334\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n335\t\n336\t### Requirement\n337\t\n338\tFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n339\t\n340\t### Verification recipe\n341\t\n342\tStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n343\t\n344\t**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n345\t\n346\t### Checks for likely_pass\n347\t\n348\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n349\t\n350\t- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n351\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n352\t- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n353\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n354\t- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n355\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n356\t- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n357\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n358\t\n359\t### Not covered here\n360\t\n361\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n362\t\n363\t- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n364\t- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n365\t- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n366\t\n367\t### Heuristics\n368\t\n369\t**Provable from code:** partly\n370\t\n371\t**Look for:**\n372\t- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n373\t- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n374\t- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n375\t- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n376\t- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n377\t\n378\t**Red flags:**\n379\t- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n380\t- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n381\t- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n382\t- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n383\t- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n384\t\n385\t**Where to look:**\n386\t- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n387\t- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n388\t- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n389\t\n390\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n391\t\n392\t**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n393\t\n394\t## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n395\t\n396\t**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n397\t\n398\t### Requirement\n399\t\n400\tList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n401\t\n402\t### Verification recipe\n403\t\n404\tList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n405\t\n406\t**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n407\t\n408\t### Checks for likely_pass\n409\t\n410\tRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n411\t\n412\t- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n413\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n414\t- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n415\t  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n416\t- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n417\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n418\t- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n419\t  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n420\t\n421\t### Not covered here\n422\t\n423\tFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n424\t\n425\t- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n426\t- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n427\t- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n428\t\n429\t### Heuristics\n430\t\n431\t**Provable from code:** partly\n432\t\n433\t**Look for:**\n434\t- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n435\t- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n436\t- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n437\t- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n438\t\n439\t**Red flags:**\n440\t- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n441\t- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n442\t- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n443\t- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n444\t- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n445\t\n446\t**Where to look:**\n447\t- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n448\t- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n449\t- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n450\t- model routing and fallback config\n451\t\n452\t**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n453\t\n454\t**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n455\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2062f5b6-954e-4243-ac14-d74aabea3af5","uuid":"196a34bf-bfb5-4272-8403-0d20a3258d91","timestamp":"2026-10-20T12:37:25.858Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-v4gibde9/skill/references/agent.md","content":"## A05 · G1 · C8/C9/Obs-T1 — Operational lookup\n\n**Lead:** Operations. **Shares:** Business owner, Governance, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nStart from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nGive an operator a test action or trace ID with no other context. Have them find the manifest, owners, and live instance, and contain that instance. Confirm the targeted run stops and unrelated test runs keep their access.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Lookup queries, drill timeline, results for target and non-target runs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A05.1** Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n- **A05.2** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n- **A05.3** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time → **R11**\n- No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type → **E12**\n- Action records missing any of the six identity fields → **A02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Telemetry links each action to type ID, instance ID, owner, and trace, so one action ID can be traced to a manifest and a live instance.\n- A registry or index mapping `agent.type.id` to manifest, owners, and running instances (service catalog entry, CODEOWNERS, `.brace/deployment.yaml` roles).\n- A kill or quarantine control scoped to one instance or one agent type (per-instance token revocation, per-type feature flag, scaling one deployment to zero).\n- A runbook that starts from a trace or action ID and lists the lookup and containment steps.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- The only stop control is a global switch or revoking a shared credential, which would cut off the launching user or other agents.\n- No mapping from runtime identifiers back to a manifest or owner team.\n- Instances are not individually addressable (no instance ID, no per-run credential).\n- Owner information lives only in a wiki with no link from telemetry.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- runbooks, incident docs, service catalog files (for example `catalog-info.yaml`)\n- admin or ops endpoints and CLIs for stopping runs\n- credential issuing code (per-instance vs shared)\n- CODEOWNERS and deployment records\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can't show an operator can do the lookup in practice or that containment hits only the target. Needs the A05 drill: start from one action or trace ID, find manifest, owners, and instance, contain it, and confirm unrelated runs keep access.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## A06 · G3 · Obs-T3 — Parent and prompt provenance\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Data and privacy, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nFor every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nStart a child and a grandchild agent, each with a test prompt you can tell apart. From the grandchild's tool action, trace the chain back to the root. Using the approved audit role, pull each prompt a parent passed down. A role without approval must not be able to read protected prompt text. Confirm prompt capture is turned on. Standard top-level agent attributes don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Nested trace, protected prompt references, access test.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A06.1** Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request.\n- **A06.2** Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID.\n- **A06.3** The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset.\n- **A06.4** Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Sub-agent given powers beyond its task → **E08**\n- Children still running after the parent is stopped → **E09**\n- No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Sub-agent spawn code assigns the child its own `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` and records a parent link (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`).\n- The prompt the parent passes is recorded as `agent.parent.prompt`, either in full or as a hash reference with the text in restricted storage.\n- Trace context is passed into the child (span parent or span link), including for remote or queued sub-agents.\n- GenAI prompt capture is turned on for this path (for example a content-capture env var or SDK option) and a redaction policy is defined.\n- Restricted storage for prompt text with access limited to an approved audit role.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Sub-agents reuse the parent's instance ID or have no IDs of their own.\n- Child agents start a fresh trace with no link to the parent span.\n- Prompt content capture is off by default and never turned on, so parent prompts are never recorded.\n- Full parent prompts with customer data are written to general-purpose logs anyone on the team can read.\n- Only top-level `gen_ai.agent.*` attributes are set, which don't identify the direct parent instance.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- sub-agent or handoff code (for example LangGraph subgraphs, OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs, Claude Agent SDK subagents, A2A task creation)\n- OpenTelemetry instrumentation settings and env vars for content capture\n- prompt storage and its access policy (bucket or table IAM)\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the A06 recipe: a nested child and grandchild trace back to the root, pulling each parent prompt with the approved role, and a denied read with an unapproved role.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\n\n## A07 · G3 · C7/Obs-T1 — Every model in the loop\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Model team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nList every model call in a typical workflow and match each to a model record. Swap a checker's version, and simulate a timeout or an invalid checker decision. The set control-failure response must happen and name the affected model.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Call list, model provenance records, failure and swap results.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **A07.1** A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n- **A07.2** Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n- **A07.3** Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n- **A07.4** A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed → **C05**\n- Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior → **R10**\n- Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check → **C05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Every model call site is listed with its role (main, judge, verifier, reranker, embedding, router, fallback) and a model record.\n- Helper model IDs, prompts, and config are pinned in config and covered by the manifest, not hard-coded inline with aliases.\n- Checker decisions are logged with the checker's model ID and version.\n- Code treats a checker timeout, error, or invalid output as a failure of the dependent control (block or escalate), not as a pass.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Judge or guardrail call wrapped in `try/except` that returns allow or skips the check on error or timeout.\n- Helper models (embedding, reranker, classifier) called with no recorded model version.\n- Checker model set to a moving alias (for example `-latest`) while the main model is pinned.\n- Unparseable checker output is defaulted to safe or approved.\n- Model routing or fallback silently switches to a different model with no record.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- all LLM client call sites (search for provider SDK clients and model name strings)\n- guardrail, judge, moderation, and evaluator modules\n- retrieval pipeline (embedding and reranker config)\n- model routing and fallback config\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code can list call sites and failure handling. The A07 recipe proves the list is complete in a real workflow and that a swapped, timed-out, or invalid checker triggers the set failure response.\n\n**N/A when:** The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\n","numLines":183,"startLine":273,"totalLines":455}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KaCC191gqmPxhz9Je5X1fk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
